<summary>inc/site-exporter/database/class-max-execution-time.php-34-41 (1)</summary><blockquote>

`34-41`: **Logic issue: `store()` only called when `$time === 0`.**

The current implementation only stores the original limit when setting to unlimited (`$time = 0`). If a caller uses `set(60)` followed by `restore()`, the restored value will be `0` (the class default) instead of the actual previous limit.

Consider always storing before setting:



<details>
<summary>🔎 Proposed fix</summary>

```diff
 public function set(int $time = 0): void {

-	if (0 === $time) {
-		$this->store();
-	}
+	$this->store();

 	@set_time_limit($time); // phpcs:ignore WordPress.PHP.NoSilencedErrors.Discouraged
 }
```
</details>

</blockquote></details>
<details>
<summary>inc/site-exporter/class-site-exporter.php-1543-1553 (1)</summary><blockquote>

`1543-1553`: **The `running` flag is set to `false` which seems incorrect.**

When marking an import as "in progress", setting `$options['running'] = false` appears to be a logic error. This should likely be `true` to indicate the import is currently running.


<details>
<summary>🔎 Proposed fix</summary>

```diff
 	if (empty($file_name)) {
 		return false;
 	}

-	$options['running'] = false;
+	$options['running'] = true;

 	$base = [
 		$file_name,
 		$options,
 		$hash,
 	];
```
</details>

</blockquote></details>
<details>
<summary>inc/site-exporter/class-site-exporter.php-1307-1330 (1)</summary><blockquote>

`1307-1330`: **`reset_upload_limits()` has security and reliability concerns.**

1. `@ini_set()` calls may silently fail if PHP is configured to ignore runtime INI changes
2. Defining `ALLOW_UNFILTERED_UPLOADS` globally is a significant security risk as it allows uploading any file type network-wide



Consider limiting the scope of `ALLOW_UNFILTERED_UPLOADS`:

```diff
-		if (! defined('ALLOW_UNFILTERED_UPLOADS')) {
-			define('ALLOW_UNFILTERED_UPLOADS', true);
-		}
+		// Use filter instead of constant for more controlled unfiltered upload access
